      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The plasma levels of brain natriuretic peptide, tumor necrosis factor-alpha, big endothelin-1 and cardiac troponins have been reported to correlate with the severity of heart failure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In a single population of 80 outpatients with mild to moderate chronic heart failure the correlation between the patient's functional capacity, as evaluated at a 6-min walk test, the clinical parameters and plasma levels of brain natriuretic peptide, tumor necrosis factor-alpha, big endothelin-1 and cardiac troponins was evaluated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A significant inverse correlation was found with the patient's age (p < 0.0001), NYHA functional class (p < 0.0001), left ventricular dysfunction etiology (ischemic vs dilated cardiomyopathy, p < 0.0005), heart rate (p < 0.05), plasma levels of brain natriuretic peptide (p < 0.05) and of tumor necrosis factor-alpha (p < 0.0005). At multiple regression analysis a correlation was found between the 6-min walk test results and the patient's age (p < 0.05), NYHA functional class (p < 0.01), left ventricular dysfunction etiology (ischemic vs dilated cardiomyopathy, p < 0.05) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha plasma levels (p < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In our patients with mild to moderate heart failure, a significant correlation was found between the results of the 6-min walk test and only the plasma concentrations of tumor necrosis factor-alpha among the laboratory parameters analyzed in this study."
        }
      ]},
